    "result_text": "The build succeeds and the only test class (`AcceptanceTest`) passes. The full suite is green.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the `customerCode` assignment rule (`<LAST3>-<NNNN>`) with each piece of logic in its proper place:\n\n**New logic \u2014 one small, single-responsibility unit**\n- `rest/validation/CustomerCodeGenerator.java` \u2014 a pure formatter that builds the code from a last name and the current owner count (`LAST3` = upper-cased first three letters; `NNNN` = zero-padded `count + 1`). This mirrors the existing `TelephoneNormalizer`/`EmailNormalizer` style.\n\n**Wiring it into the create flow (mirrors existing normalization there)**\n- `OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner` now assigns the code just before saving, sourcing the count from the service. It runs only on create, so `updateOwner` never rewrites it.\n\n**Counting owners (reused down the existing layers)**\n- `OwnerRepository.count()` added to the interface, implemented in the JPA and JDBC impls; the Spring Data repository (the profile the tests use) gets it for free as a standard derived method.\n- `ClinicService.countOwners()` delegates to the repository, consistent with the facade's other methods.\n\n**Persistence & exposure of the field**\n- `Owner` entity: added the `customerCode` field mapped to a new `customer_code` column, with getter/setter.\n- `openapi.yml`: added `customerCode` as a `readOnly` property on the `Owner` schema, so the generated DTO carries it and MapStruct maps it by name into `GET` responses automatically.\n- Added the `customer_code` column to all four DB schemas (hsqldb, h2, postgres, mysql); it's nullable so the existing seed data still loads.\n\nThe acceptance test's key checks hold: codes are prefixed `SMI-`/`JON-`, and because the count is read immediately before each insert (auto-flushed within the transaction), the second owner's sequence is exactly one greater than the first.",
